1. Security Hardening
    - Security hardening in Kubernetes focuses on reducing the attack surface, enforcing least privilege, and protecting workloads, images, and the software supply chain.

2. Pod Security Standards (PSS)
    - Pod Security Standards define security levels for Pods and replace the older PodSecurityPolicy mechanism.
    - Levels
    1. Privileged
        Unrestricted policy
        Allows privileged containers and host access
        Suitable only for trusted system workloads
    2. Baseline
        Prevents known privilege escalations
        Disallows dangerous capabilities
        Suitable for most applications
    3. Restricted
        Enforces strict security controls
        Requires non-root execution
        Limits Linux capabilities
        Recommended for production workloads
    - Example Namespace Labels
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: Namespace
        metadata:
            name: production
            labels:
                pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
                pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
                pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted

3. Security Contexts
    - Security contexts define privilege and access control settings for Pods or Containers.
    - Common Security Settings
        Run containers as non-root
        Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities
        Use read-only root filesystem
        Prevent privilege escalation
    - Example
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: Pod
        metadata:
            name: secure-pod
        spec:
            securityContext:
                runAsNonRoot: true
                runAsUser: 1000
        containers:
            - name: app
                image: nginx
                securityContext:
                    allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
                    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
                    capabilities:
                    drop:
                        - ALL
    - Important Fields
        Field	                    Purpose
        runAsNonRoot	            Prevents root execution
        runAsUser	                Specifies container UID
        readOnlyRootFilesystem	    Prevents filesystem modification
        allowPrivilegeEscalation	Blocks privilege escalation
        capabilities.drop	        Removes Linux capabilities

4. Image Security
    - Container images must be secured to reduce vulnerabilities.
    - Best Practices
        Use minimal base images
            Alpine
            Distroless
        Avoid running as root
        Pin image versions
        Regularly scan images for vulnerabilities
        Remove unused packages and tools
        Sign and verify images
    - Example
        FROM node:22-alpine
        WORKDIR /app
        COPY package*.json ./
        RUN npm ci --only=production
        COPY . .
        USER node
        CMD ["node", "server.js"]
    - Image Scanning Tools
        Trivy
        Clair
        Grype
        Docker Scout

5. Supply Chain Security
    - Supply chain security protects the integrity of software artifacts throughout the build and deployment lifecycle.
    - Key Areas
    1. Artifact Signing
        Verify image authenticity
        Prevent tampered images
    2. SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)
        Track dependencies
        Improve vulnerability management
    3. CI/CD Security
        Protect secrets
        Use least-privileged runners
        Verify dependencies
    4. Admission Control
        Block untrusted images
        Enforce policies before deployment
    - Common Tools
        Cosign — image signing and verification
        Syft — generates SBOMs
        OPA Gatekeeper — policy enforcement
        Kyverno — Kubernetes-native policies
    - Example Cosign Verification
        cosign verify nginx:latest